SSH user certificate CA

Files
  ssh_ca_key: CA private key for signing user login certificates. Keep it off devices.
  ssh_ca_key.pub: CA public key installed at /etc/ssh/ssh_ca_key.pub on devices.
  When using an existing CA, use the public key matching its private key.
  This CA is independent of the OTA signing certificate and secure boot keys.

Generate a CA
  Run these commands in a new, empty directory:

  umask 077
  ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ssh_ca_key -N '' -C 'NAOS SSH user CA'
  chmod 600 ssh_ca_key

Issue a login certificate
  Generate a client key, then sign its public key with the CA private key.
  This example allows root and naos logins for 52 weeks:

  ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f client-key -N '' -C 'NAOS administrator'
  ssh-keygen -s ssh_ca_key -I naos-admin -n root,naos -V +52w client-key.pub

  Use client-key-cert.pub together with the client private key:

  ssh -i client-key -o CertificateFile=client-key-cert.pub root@DEVICE_IP

  The certificate principals must include the login username. Keep the client
  private key and CA private key on the administrator's machine. Devices need
  only the CA public key.
